 training  and education and yet this is not the  book Walmart was hoping he'd write just  look at the title still broke did you  wrestle with that one I think the  company still broke after all the change  that is made on this front it hasn't  fixed things yet  to make that case wardsman uses the very  same numbers that Walmart sites with  pride including an average wage that's  now more than 17 dollars an hour higher  than the minimums in every state in  America so what's the problem  well the problem is we've kind of  Forgotten what people actually need to  live on that 17 an hour works out to an  average annual income of just under 29  000 a year at the end of the day after  all this change after all the pushings  and internal conversations and internal  soul-searching that's not a living wage  and the problem he says is far bigger  than just Walmart what this has shown me  just by looking at Walmart is that  Corporate America overall will never go  far enough fast enough on its own the  only solution wardsman says is a federal  intervention a new national minimum wage  of twenty dollars an hour almost triple  the current level we've let things go  for so long and haven't dealt with the  problem  that now we're our backs are against the  wall we don't have time for  incrementalism anymore Walmart's CEO  agrees that at least some increase is  required the federal minimum wage is  lagging behind seven dollars and 25  cents is too low but 20 or even 15 is so  high According to some economists it  could backfire last year for example the  non-partisan Congressional budget office  concluded that raising the minimum to 15  an hour by 2025 would indeed live some  900 000 people out of poverty but would  result in higher prices leading to fewer  sales and resulting in companies  reducing their employment in the end it  estimated 1.4 million Americans would  lose their jobs so the trade-off is yeah  you help a bunch of people make a little  bit more but a whole other bunch of  people are going to be out of a job  entirely and what about them yeah I I  don't buy that they're always going to  be trade-offs there is no perfect  solution to this we've dug ourselves a  hole over 50 years  again we've got to do something Walmart  argues It's Already Done quite a lot in  recent years the bottom line is if  Walmart were not in operation in this  country there would be more people on  public assistance not less Dan Bartlett  is one of the company's top Executives  this is a Walmart Academy something that  didn't even exist if you know five six  seven years ago he wanted to show us a  training facility near the company's  headquarters in Northwest Arkansas where  workers can pick up skills to help them  at Walmart and Beyond what we're  providing to them is enormous amount of  opportunity for them to grow with the  company to make more money and to them  they have to decide then is what is the  wage they need to provide for themselves  or a family we're going to give them an  opportunity to grow but as Walmart  pledges to keep raising pay according to  its website Rick says it already has the  money to do it pointing to the tens of  billions of dollars spent in recent  years buying back its own stock a move  that typically drives up share prices  why not take a fraction of that stock  buyback money and just give people a  more decent living so when we look at  our shareholders who by the way we have  tens of thousands of employees who who  are invested in our own stock and 401k  and those things so they benefit from  those share repurchases as well the  simplistic answer for Rick is that  increasing dramatically the minimum wage  to twenty dollars will solve all these  problems I think is misguided and the  ramifications of there'll be job  destruction I could tell to you today  that if we took overnight all of our  stores in Walmart to twenty dollars  there would be hundreds of stores that  would no longer be profitable so  potential  or do you have to decide to or lay  people off or raise prices  and so you start raising prices you're  hurting communities they're the very  ones you that Rick says he cares most  about maybe so says wartsman but  whatever the trade-offs of a twenty  dollar minimum wage he's sure they would  beat the reality of right now and you  can see that this is going to sound  radical to a lot of people and I have to  admit it does sound like more than is  possible politically you can say twenty  dollars an hour is radical what's  radical to me is to be in the richest  country on Earth and have people who get  up and work hard every Day Struggle to  the degree they are  and there it is and there it is it's  well said I mean it's 40 to 60 million  people by most estimates who get up work  very hard every day and still make  trade-offs at the end of the month  between food and rent you know utilities  and food and it's a hard life let me ask  you something because I know that you do  your research before sitting down with  anybody where does Walmart stand in  comparison to Target or someone like  Amazon I don't know the exact  head-to-heads about those individual  companies but Walmart has made a real  and significant investment in improving  things for its workers and so has much  of corporate America but you see when  you take 17 an hour and you have a 36  hour weekly that's a full-time worker  it's twenty nine thousand dollars people  sitting on their couch at home wherever  you're living ask yourself does twenty  nine thousand dollars get you far and I  think the folks sitting on the couch  look to those CEO and top-tier level  svps EVPs and look at their salaries and  their bonuses and say well maybe those  need to come down and like spread some  of the wealth and it's easy for you know  anyone on the outside to look at Walmart  and say you're highly profitable the  execs are making money Millions  sometimes billions yeah why don't you  spread that money among the people that  